#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Rotate the Talos API CA — the only way to revoke a leaked talosconfig"
set -euo pipefail

# Talos has no CRL: a leaked client certificate cannot be revoked, and issuing a
# replacement admin cert leaves the leaked one working until it expires. Rotating
# the CA is what invalidates it. See docs/runbooks/talos-ca-rotation.md — read it
# before running this, particularly step 4, which the upstream docs do not cover.

node="${1:-}"
if [[ -z "$node" ]]; then
  echo "usage: mise run talos-rotate-ca <control-plane-ip>" >&2
  echo "       e.g. mise run talos-rotate-ca 10.10.206.14" >&2
  exit 2
fi

echo "==> Dry run against ${node}. Nothing changes yet."
echo
talosctl -n "$node" rotate-ca --dry-run=true --talos=true --kubernetes=false

cat <<'WARN'

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Read the plan above before continuing.

This rotates the Talos API CA only (--kubernetes=false). A leaked
talosconfig is a Talos-API credential; the Kubernetes CA is a separate
rotation with its own blast radius. Rotate that too only if a kubeconfig
leaked.

After it completes you MUST:
  - talosctl config merge ./talosconfig   (and delete the local copy)
  - write the new CA into talos/talsecret.sops.yaml

That second one is not optional and not in the upstream docs. talhelper
renders machine configs from talsecret, and `rotate-ca` cannot update it
(siderolabs/talos#12816). Leave it stale and the next `genconfig` pushes
the OLD CA back, undoing this.

Anyone holding a talosconfig you have not recorded loses access.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

WARN

read -r -p "Type 'rotate' to proceed, anything else to abort: " reply
if [[ "$reply" != "rotate" ]]; then
  echo "Aborted. Nothing was changed."
  exit 1
fi

echo "==> Rotating."
talosctl -n "$node" rotate-ca --dry-run=false --talos=true --kubernetes=false

cat <<'NEXT'

==> Rotation command finished. It is NOT remediated until you have:

  1. talosctl config merge ./talosconfig   &&  rm ./talosconfig
  2. Updated certs.os.{crt,key} in talos/talsecret.sops.yaml (sops, in place)
  3. Confirmed talhelper genconfig now renders the NEW CA
  4. Proven the old credential is dead:
       talosctl --talosconfig ./old-talosconfig -n <node> version   # must FAIL

Step 4 is the gate. See docs/runbooks/talos-ca-rotation.md.
NEXT
